course: dictionary
lesson: 8376
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word row is a good example. «Theo lost three races in a row and threw the controller onto the sofa.» Here row means one after the other, with no break. It does not mean a line or a quarrel.
explain: The same word can be about a boat. «They watch him splash past, rolling badly but rowing his hardest.» Toad is moving a boat through the water with oars, so rowing means using oars. Ask yourself: what would make sense in this sentence?
explain: Row can also be a noisy quarrel, and it is said like «cow». «In Birmingham, after a row about homework, Dhruv's mum shouts and his dad says nothing all evening.» Here a row is an argument. If you say «row» like «cow» and it means an angry quarrel, you are using the right meaning.
fact: Row (rhymes with go) means a line of things or people side by side, like «a row of chairs».
fact: To row means to move a boat through water using oars.
fact: «In a row» means one after the other, with no break.
fact: Row (said like «cow») means a noisy quarrel, and to row means to argue loudly. This is used in UK, Irish and Australian English.
fact: In a table or spreadsheet, a row goes across and a column goes down.
exercise: «Theo lost three races in a row and threw the controller onto the sofa.» What does in a row mean? | A line of chairs side by side | *One after the other, with no break | A loud argument | Moving through water with oars
exercise: «Toad is rowing his hardest» in a little boat. What is rowing? | Arguing loudly with a friend | Adding numbers in columns | *Moving a boat through water using oars | A straight line of people
exercise: In Birmingham, Dhruv's mum and dad had a row about homework. What is a row here? | A line of seats in a classroom | A list of numbers going down a page | *A noisy quarrel | A trip out on a lake
test: In a table or spreadsheet, which way does a row go? | Diagonally | *Across | Down | In a circle
test: «The front row of the class was ready for the test.» What does row mean here? | A noisy quarrel | Something that is rowed in a boat | *A line of seats side by side | A tough job to do
exam: Which formal word means a quarrel, as in «a row about homework»? | *an altercation | a tier | consecutively | a linear arrangement
